#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078261 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74a902da-f299-54a3-8e93-ad2716d0e887" :knowledge-point/title "Modeling Inhibited Decay"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8012ecda-d2f4-52de-99de-7b2ebd635661" :question/math-academy-id "e-6676" :question/problem "A loaf of bread just taken out of the oven has a temperature of $180^{\\circ}\\textrm{C}.$ After placing it on the table, the temperature $T(t)$ of the bread decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling. Here, $T$ is measured in degrees Celsius, and $t > 0$ is the time in minutes. If the room temperature is $25^{\\circ}\\textrm{C},$ which differential equation could be used to model the bread's temperature? Assume that $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d T}{\\textrm d t} = -k(T - T_S),\n$$\n\nwhere $T_S$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $T_S = 25^{\\circ}\\textrm{C},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d T}{\\textrm d t} = -k(T - 25).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078262 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bfe4679b-9724-5502-89d4-05e7661a1b5f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inhibited Decay Model"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e3e001a8-9294-59a4-82e6-b1122afdc72f" :question/math-academy-id "e-6677" :question/problem "The temperature $T(t),$ in degrees Celsius, of a fluorescent light bulb after turning off can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}T}{\\mathrm{d}t} =  -0.25(T-23)\n$$\n\nwhere $t > 0$ is the time, measured in minutes. If the initial temperature of the bulb is $80^\\circ \\rm C,$ calculate the temperature of the bulb after $4$ minutes. Round your answer to the nearest integer." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "We're told that the temperature of the fluorescent light bulb is modeled by the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}T}{\\mathrm{d}t} =  -0.25(T-23).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-23} & =-0.25\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-23} & =-\\int 0.25\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-23| & =-0.25t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T - 23 > 0$ for all $t > 0.$ \nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-23 & ={e}^{-0.25t+C} \\\\ T-23 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-0.25t} \\\\ T-23 & =K{e}^{-0.25t} \\\\ T & =23+K{e}^{-0.25t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K=e^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature of the light bulb was $80^\\circ \\rm C.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=80,$ and solve for $K{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}80 & =23+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 80 & =23+K \\\\ K & =57\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\nT(t) = 23+57e^{-0.25t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the temperature of the bulb after $t=4$ minutes is\n\n$$\nT(4) = 23+57e^{-0.25\\cdot 4}\\approx 44^\\circ\\textrm{C},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078295 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0709c667-bc10-5bd5-a383-3fd6ca332361" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Inhibited Decay Equations and Initial Value Problems"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9d7fbff5-7db5-53e4-a17e-3fb7ac7a0069" :question/math-academy-id "e-8957" :question/problem "Given that $y > 50$ and $t > 0,$ which of the following is an inhibited decay equation?\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} =600+12y \\[5pt]$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} =600-12y \\[5pt]$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} =600-12y^2$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "An inhibited decay equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = -k(y - a),\n$$\n\nwhere $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $y > a$ for all $t > 0.$\n\nFrom the given options, II is the only inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} =600-12y.\n$$\n\nTo see this, let's factor the right-hand side of the equation. This gives,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = -12(y-50).\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=12,$ and $a=50.$ Note that for inhibited decay, we must have $y > 50$ for all $t > 0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078623 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0709c667-bc10-5bd5-a383-3fd6ca332361" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Inhibited Decay Equations and Initial Value Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85b8d3c4-45c3-57c2-9403-a1b0981cf83d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt} =-2y(y - 500), y(0) = 400" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075048} {:answer/id #uuid "a0638072-af92-5e17-b6b1-6a6036e0f9c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt} = 2(y - 500), y(0) = 600" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075049} {:answer/id #uuid "d5852a94-6331-5384-a173-629cde50b956" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt} = 2y(y - 500), y(0) = 600" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075050} {:answer/id #uuid "26c656e9-eb6a-526d-8a67-e22c14b1c437" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt} =-2(y - 500), y(0) = 400" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075051} {:answer/id #uuid "5aa4aaae-b654-5e69-b571-27ea8068bf99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt} =-2(y - 500), y(0) = 600" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075052}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5aa4aaae-b654-5e69-b571-27ea8068bf99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt} =-2(y - 500), y(0) = 600" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075052} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1233007-f334-5344-aa80-675719f92937" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075047}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7fb765b0-7266-558a-8445-d5e7e8dcfb07" :question/math-academy-id "q-105890" :question/problem "Which of the following initial value problems is an inhibited decay problem?" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "An inhibited decay initial value problem has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-k(y-a),\\,y(0)={y}_{0}\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $y>a$ for all $t>0.$\n\nFrom the given options, the only inhibited decay problem is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-2(y-500),\\,y(0)=600.\n$$\n\nIn this case, $k=2,a=500,$ and ${y}_{0}=600.$ Notice that ${y}_{0}>a,$ which is required for inhibited decay. \n\nThe other options do not represent inhibited decay. In particular, the initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-2(y-500),\\,y(0)=400,\n$$\n\ndoes *not* represent inhibited decay model because ${y}_{0}=400<a=500.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078625 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0709c667-bc10-5bd5-a383-3fd6ca332361" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Inhibited Decay Equations and Initial Value Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86bbab44-6069-592a-a4a7-b0b0db3dcec5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt} = 80(y + 20)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075060} {:answer/id #uuid "c13c00a7-95a3-5962-b866-cb920a5a6130" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt} =-80(y - 20)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075061} {:answer/id #uuid "eb974e6a-510e-5517-9887-f1543ffb7ca3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt} =-80y(20 - y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075062} {:answer/id #uuid "1333b2f7-f369-5e68-b5d9-35050d676786" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt} =-80(20 - y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075063} {:answer/id #uuid "537e490d-a5c5-5410-b145-61849298df4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt} = 80y(y + 20)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075064}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c13c00a7-95a3-5962-b866-cb920a5a6130" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt} =-80(y - 20)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075061} :answer-field/id #uuid "7710f67b-7902-53e9-9bb6-4dac11bb3641" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075059}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f0fe545c-1008-5ec6-a573-a706bc6b026b" :question/math-academy-id "q-105898" :question/problem "Given that $y>20$ and $t>0,$ which of the following is an inhibited decay equation?" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "An inhibited decay equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-k(y-a),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $y>a$ for all $t>0.$\n\nFrom the given options, the only inhibited decay equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-80(y-20).\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=80,$ and $a=20.$ Note that for inhibited decay, we must have $y>20$ for all $t>0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321777 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0709c667-bc10-5bd5-a383-3fd6ca332361" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Inhibited Decay Equations and Initial Value Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53a73152-5784-5553-8b38-3c07e4803fdd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-5(9-y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321779} {:answer/id #uuid "ad4132ea-0437-5fbd-9d1c-ddd3bef30296" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-5(y-9)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321780} {:answer/id #uuid "3b9f6d46-aa7e-5027-8e60-1470e12c1922" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-5{y}^{2}(y-9)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321781} {:answer/id #uuid "0e0315b9-dbdb-59bd-a0dc-d84f6a8a3222" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-5({y}^{2}-9)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321782} {:answer/id #uuid "0078e3b7-1e8d-55ab-ae0d-c398206fd4e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-5y(y+9)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321783}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ad4132ea-0437-5fbd-9d1c-ddd3bef30296" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-5(y-9)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321780} :answer-field/id #uuid "894a2987-4984-5afc-a234-5be706772146" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321778}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4ab835de-6077-5001-a0a7-4516bf0305aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-106647" :question/problem "Given that $y>9$ and $t>0,$ which of the following is an inhibited decay equation?" :question/worked-solution "An inhibited decay equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-k(y-a),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $y>a$ for all $t>0.$\n\nFrom the given options, the only inhibited decay equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-5(y-9).\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=5,$ and $a=9.$ Note that for inhibited decay, we must have $y>9$ for all $t>0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321784 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0709c667-bc10-5bd5-a383-3fd6ca332361" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Inhibited Decay Equations and Initial Value Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f60b8ad-9042-5d96-9494-00fc2a99d0ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=176+22y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321786} {:answer/id #uuid "1adcf22b-d4aa-5448-a4f8-41d1692e6986" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=176-22{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321787} {:answer/id #uuid "fbd69f58-d216-5fe1-a473-713e92d34eb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=176-22y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321788} {:answer/id #uuid "765a6d12-c1f7-5aa6-bf59-1426da2349d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=22{y}^{2}-176" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321789} {:answer/id #uuid "cec4a4c4-579c-592b-bba7-9bae2d3352e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=176y-22{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321790}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fbd69f58-d216-5fe1-a473-713e92d34eb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=176-22y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321788} :answer-field/id #uuid "388d11f6-f844-5c48-85f2-2e7770c48438" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321785}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "31f331f4-2197-5cc1-8e14-b967466e677c" :question/math-academy-id "q-106651" :question/problem "Given that $y>8$ and $t>0,$ which of the following is an inhibited decay equation?" :question/worked-solution "An inhibited decay equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-k(y-a),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $y>a$ for all $t>0.$\n\nFrom the given options, the only inhibited decay equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=176-22y.\n$$\n\nTo see this, let's factor the right-hand side of the equation. This gives,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-22(y-8).\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=22,$ and $a=8.$ Note that for inhibited decay, we must have $y>8$ for all $t>0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321791 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0709c667-bc10-5bd5-a383-3fd6ca332361" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Inhibited Decay Equations and Initial Value Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "75c50c6a-85f9-5fc6-a66c-431f9860354e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=28{y}^{2}-840" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321793} {:answer/id #uuid "3809d0a6-7a4f-59f3-90c1-7eb586d58bfe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=840y-28{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321794} {:answer/id #uuid "a2206494-11d5-5ec6-8fdd-efc01cc18ab3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=840-28y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321795} {:answer/id #uuid "263f5cda-4bff-5ace-aae0-cccf03212642" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=840+28y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321796} {:answer/id #uuid "cfa8f299-e65c-5829-9816-73ac95593958" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=840-28{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321797}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a2206494-11d5-5ec6-8fdd-efc01cc18ab3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=840-28y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321795} :answer-field/id #uuid "887bbde7-4844-5579-8445-8e33a034a40e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321792}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6f61f141-3dc0-53a6-8909-845a088a0525" :question/math-academy-id "q-106654" :question/problem "Given that $y>30$ and $t>0,$ which of the following is an inhibited decay equation?" :question/worked-solution "An inhibited decay equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-k(y-a),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $y>a$ for all $t>0.$\n\nFrom the given options, the only inhibited decay equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=840-28y.\n$$\n\nTo see this, let's factor the right-hand side of the equation. This gives,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-28(y-30).\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=28,$ and $a=30.$ Note that for inhibited decay, we must have $y>30$ for all $t>0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321798 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74a902da-f299-54a3-8e93-ad2716d0e887" :knowledge-point/title "Modeling Inhibited Decay"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a9649ba-4862-587a-bf6e-a2d1ad382097" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{T}^{′}(t)=kT(T-21)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321800} {:answer/id #uuid "4a2dfc7a-9d22-5686-907d-5ee45dc13e62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{T}^{′}(t)=-kT(T-21)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321801} {:answer/id #uuid "d0823d2d-8ba9-5877-9097-88a6eb26c7fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{T}^{′}(t)=-k(T-21)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321802} {:answer/id #uuid "87cdb677-a03a-55d2-99f2-3df8718e2fbd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{T}^{′}(t)=k(T+21)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321803} {:answer/id #uuid "ccb0c620-d9ba-57be-82c4-03d7cfde7891" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{T}^{′}(t)=-k(T+21)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321804}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d0823d2d-8ba9-5877-9097-88a6eb26c7fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{T}^{′}(t)=-k(T-21)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321802} :answer-field/id #uuid "b526f018-bed6-5df3-b8fd-2fe84f17f75e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321799}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d44d9a6b-035e-5168-a3f6-72ab2e21a7d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-106878" :question/problem "The rate at which a body cools is proportional to the difference between its temperature and the temperature of its surroundings. Let $T(t)$ be the temperature of the body in degrees Celsius, and let $t>0$ be the time in minutes. If the temperature of the surroundings is ${21}^{∘}\\text{C},$ which differential equation could be used to model the temperature of the liquid? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={21}^{∘}\\text{C},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-21).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321805 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74a902da-f299-54a3-8e93-ad2716d0e887" :knowledge-point/title "Modeling Inhibited Decay"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ab9ecb8-1147-57e8-ba45-5d8467837473" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-145(T-67)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321807} {:answer/id #uuid "99c3be4b-8a21-5952-aede-bcab6df0f70a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-145)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321808} {:answer/id #uuid "a2cf0719-7956-5878-a0a3-2147926ec997" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-kT(T-67)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321809} {:answer/id #uuid "9479a20c-670b-5663-a4ae-883b85314182" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-67)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321810} {:answer/id #uuid "7ccacb23-d5e4-5e9e-a1d6-012e7812d625" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-67(T-145)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321811}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9479a20c-670b-5663-a4ae-883b85314182" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-67)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321810} :answer-field/id #uuid "81ace175-236e-54be-89b4-4ae677e9abb1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321806}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "998f64be-ba94-50b9-9031-1ab4cbc445c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-106883" :question/problem "A turkey reaches ${145}^{∘}\\text{F}$ in the oven. After removing the turkey from the oven, the turkey's temperature $T(t)$ decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling. Here, $T$ is measured in degrees Fahrenheit, and $t>0$ is the time in minutes. If the room temperature is ${67}^{∘}\\text{F},$ which differential equation could be used to model the temperature of the turkey? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={67}^{∘}\\text{F},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-67).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321812 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74a902da-f299-54a3-8e93-ad2716d0e887" :knowledge-point/title "Modeling Inhibited Decay"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e58bc144-a31b-5693-8226-a571d4a9e5dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-200(T-76)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321814} {:answer/id #uuid "c0e1f97d-4508-5256-9834-5b5e15f446c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-76)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321815} {:answer/id #uuid "23e5ff6a-a241-5e88-8d8c-faa8c693b1d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=200k(T-76)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321816} {:answer/id #uuid "d57b74ed-1d51-5b75-9d79-c744e71f8cbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-76k(T-200)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321817} {:answer/id #uuid "81336641-e23f-5bc1-ae99-abfdc664c303" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-200)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321818}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c0e1f97d-4508-5256-9834-5b5e15f446c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-76)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321815} :answer-field/id #uuid "cee60ca1-f74e-5828-b592-784fe62efe21" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321813}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0759ede7-6062-5a98-ab6f-920712fd67b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-106934" :question/problem "In a restaurant kitchen, a pot of soup reaches a temperature of ${200}^{∘}\\text{F}.$ After turning off the stove, the temperature $T(t)$ of the soup decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling. Here, $T$ is measured in degrees Fahrenheit, and $t>0$ is the time in minutes. If the kitchen temperature is ${76}^{∘}\\text{F},$ which differential equation could be used to model the pot temperature? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={76}^{∘}\\text{F},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-76).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321819 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74a902da-f299-54a3-8e93-ad2716d0e887" :knowledge-point/title "Modeling Inhibited Decay"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6191616a-cb79-54d3-83b4-11b1fcf49e21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-90(T-19)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321821} {:answer/id #uuid "1f510184-ea1e-5a55-9996-200671c6d31f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-90k(T-19)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321822} {:answer/id #uuid "c0c1a33e-4340-5e63-aa54-83440875ed46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-90)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321823} {:answer/id #uuid "13652fb1-f84d-590d-8220-90a32d70b95b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-19k(T-90)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321824} {:answer/id #uuid "cc7520b1-5d34-588d-b6c7-d81d8a574822" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-19)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321825}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cc7520b1-5d34-588d-b6c7-d81d8a574822" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-19)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321825} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d3c5875-313e-56dc-bd36-dd119157f06e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321820}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ae2e994f-7344-5287-a621-1bc5f34b7d82" :question/math-academy-id "q-106935" :question/problem "A container of water at a temperature of ${90}^{∘}\\text{C}$ is placed in a room. The temperature $T(t)$ of the water decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling. Here, $T$ is measured in degrees Celsius, and $t>0$ is the time in minutes. If the room temperature is ${19}^{∘}\\text{C},$ which differential equation could be used to model the water temperature? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={19}^{∘}\\text{C},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-19).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321826 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bfe4679b-9724-5502-89d4-05e7661a1b5f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inhibited Decay Model"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "327b36d3-819c-5438-8676-c37d16c08a13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{96}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321828} {:answer/id #uuid "d26ecd7d-434d-58ba-932e-05a9fcbcd237" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{76}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321829} {:answer/id #uuid "e40cf18d-65d9-52e2-8fbc-73e0e1d53b21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{79}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321830} {:answer/id #uuid "5edb4d4e-1d84-5cd0-bd44-852653396e18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{81}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321831} {:answer/id #uuid "ac834029-7b61-5c03-a6e6-7240c3cc3cef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{92}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321832}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "327b36d3-819c-5438-8676-c37d16c08a13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{96}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321828} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc2435ba-70d9-51e5-b442-284e42d013fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321827}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6b4e1aaa-5dbc-5088-a3aa-1be98695474d" :question/math-academy-id "q-107118" :question/problem "The temperature $T(t),$ in degrees Celsius, of a cooling iron can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.1(T-28)\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, measured in minutes. If the initial temperature of the iron is ${140}^{∘}C,$ calculate its temperature after $5$ minutes. Round your answer to the nearest integer." :question/worked-solution "We're told that the temperature of the cooling iron is modeled by the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.1(T-28).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-28} & =-0.1\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-28} & =-\\int 0.1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-28| & =-0.1t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-28>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-28 & ={e}^{-0.1t+C} \\\\ T-28 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-0.1t} \\\\ T-28 & =K{e}^{-0.1t} \\\\ T & =28+K{e}^{-0.1t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature of the iron was ${140}^{∘}C.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=140,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}140 & =28+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 140 & =28+K \\\\ K & =112\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\nT(t)=28+112{e}^{-0.1t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the temperature of the iron after $t=5$ minutes is\n\n$$\nT(5)=28+112{e}^{-0.1\\cdot 5}≈{96}^{∘}\\text{C},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321833 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bfe4679b-9724-5502-89d4-05e7661a1b5f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inhibited Decay Model"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52cdde1c-82d5-5b64-b35a-706e91dbd8e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{189}^{∘}\\text{F}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321835} {:answer/id #uuid "7371e460-d8f2-57cb-a9d9-2aca752430dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{198}^{∘}\\text{F}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321836} {:answer/id #uuid "673f9fdd-d45e-5e35-9a8f-8fadfbef6844" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{201}^{∘}\\text{F}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321837} {:answer/id #uuid "6e67afe9-ad00-5c70-ab76-7a59e39a896b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{223}^{∘}\\text{F}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321838} {:answer/id #uuid "81fb3f4d-5f1f-535b-a29d-b61b1391cb75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{194}^{∘}\\text{F}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321839}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "673f9fdd-d45e-5e35-9a8f-8fadfbef6844" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{201}^{∘}\\text{F}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321837} :answer-field/id #uuid "a791edc1-78b5-56ba-b67e-872ee0d0c530" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321834}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "59bdbd4d-7a55-5d08-9c2c-8e53aca29431" :question/math-academy-id "q-107120" :question/problem "The temperature $T(t),$ in degrees Fahrenheit, of a fresh-baked cake can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.09(T-70)\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, measured in minutes. If the initial temperature of the cake is ${340}^{∘}F,$ calculate the temperature of the cake after $8$ minutes. Round your answer to the nearest integer." :question/worked-solution "We're told that the temperature of the fresh-baked cake is modeled by the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.09(T-70).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-70} & =-0.09\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-70} & =-\\int 0.09\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-70| & =-0.09t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-70>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-70 & ={e}^{-0.09t+C} \\\\ T-70 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-0.09t} \\\\ T-70 & =K{e}^{-0.09t} \\\\ T & =70+K{e}^{-0.09t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature of the cake was ${340}^{∘}F.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=340,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}340 & =70+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 340 & =70+K \\\\ K & =270\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\nT(t)=70+270{e}^{-0.09t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the temperature of the cake after $t=8$ minutes is\n\n$$\nT(8)=70+270{e}^{-0.09\\cdot 8}≈{201}^{∘}\\text{F},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321840 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bfe4679b-9724-5502-89d4-05e7661a1b5f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inhibited Decay Model"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "409ba1ce-74ab-529c-bfb5-77536e76250e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "23\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321842} {:answer/id #uuid "d5a03b42-d1df-5bf4-8b47-350715fdaf9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "31\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321843} {:answer/id #uuid "a7b8f96d-9a1f-5c51-8110-e9943a24685c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "45\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321844} {:answer/id #uuid "178fd248-dc39-513e-aaec-c1e0517f04f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "34\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321845} {:answer/id #uuid "ef570b0b-4090-5e12-a6ba-fbc3677ffc3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321846}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "178fd248-dc39-513e-aaec-c1e0517f04f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "34\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321845} :answer-field/id #uuid "7443f0d6-6378-52ac-8682-db6bd720f948" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321841}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fee906ee-def7-5194-a87e-a1f77108aa56" :question/math-academy-id "q-107125" :question/problem "Simon measures the temperature of the tea in his cup, and the thermometer displays a reading of ${60}^{∘}C.$ After $10$ minutes, Simon repeats the measurement and the thermometer displays a reading of  ${52}^{∘}C.$ To the nearest minute, how long does it take for the tea to decrease its temperature to ${40}^{∘}C$ if the temperature in the room is ${28}^{∘}C?$ \n\n*Hint: You may assume that the temperature $T(t)$ of the tea in Simon's cup decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling, where $T$ is measured in degrees Celsius and $t>0$ is the time, in minutes, since the first measurement.*" :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={28}^{∘}\\text{C},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-28).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-28} & =-k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-28} & =-k\\int dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-28| & =-kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-28>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-28 & ={e}^{-kt+C} \\\\ T-28 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T-28 & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T & =28+K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature reading was ${60}^{∘}C.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=60,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}60 & =28+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 60 & =28+K \\\\ K & =32\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $T(t):$\n\n$$\nT(t)=28+32{e}^{-kt}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, after $10$ minutes, the thermometer reading is ${52}^{∘}C.$ So, applying the condition $T(10)=52,$ we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}52 & =28+32{e}^{-10k} \\\\ 24 & =32{e}^{-10k} \\\\ \\frac{3}{4} & ={e}^{-10k} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{4}) & =-10k \\\\ k & =-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the temperature of the tea is ${40}^{∘}C.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({t}_{1}) & =40 \\\\ 28+32{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =40 \\\\ 32{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =12 \\\\ {e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{3}{8} \\\\ -k{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{8}) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{8}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in our value for $k,$ we conclude that the tea will have a temperature of ${40}^{∘}C$ after\n\n$$\n{t}_{1}=\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{8})}{-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})}≈34\\,\\text{min},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest minute."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321847 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bfe4679b-9724-5502-89d4-05e7661a1b5f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inhibited Decay Model"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1aa5f8e7-ec62-5131-9299-59a9b45fea82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{31}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321849} {:answer/id #uuid "a9bb5d32-72cd-58e5-879d-f1cc274c62fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{43}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321850} {:answer/id #uuid "d48b804c-44be-5140-bb6f-e1fdac43fdf3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{58}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321851} {:answer/id #uuid "a0121b34-eb76-52da-ba6d-2b1b3c087d09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{38}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321852} {:answer/id #uuid "f6bd7f09-58df-5306-8c07-0d976c872560" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{29}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321853}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0121b34-eb76-52da-ba6d-2b1b3c087d09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{38}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321852} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b546eac-0f98-51fa-8f14-4ed027e28fc9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321848}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "166a478b-a216-5747-8d39-a0615baddbf0" :question/math-academy-id "q-107129" :question/problem "The temperature $T(t),$ in degrees Celsius, of a compressor of a refrigerator can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.12(T-21)\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, measured in minutes. If the initial temperature of the compressor is ${65}^{∘}C,$ calculate the temperature of the compressor after $8$ minutes. Round your answer to the nearest integer." :question/worked-solution "We're told that the temperature of the compressor of a refrigerator is modeled by the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.12(T-21).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-21} & =-0.12\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-21} & =-\\int 0.12\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-21| & =-0.12t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-21>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-21 & ={e}^{-0.12t+C} \\\\ T-21 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-0.12t} \\\\ T-21 & =K{e}^{-0.12t} \\\\ T & =21+K{e}^{-0.12t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature of the compressor was ${65}^{∘}C.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=65,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}65 & =21+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 65 & =21+K \\\\ K & =44\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\nT(t)=21+44{e}^{-0.12t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the temperature of the compressor after $t=8$ minutes is\n\n$$\nT(8)=21+44{e}^{-0.12\\cdot 8}≈{38}^{∘}\\text{C},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321854 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bfe4679b-9724-5502-89d4-05e7661a1b5f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inhibited Decay Model"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f39fce48-2643-5117-bdc9-074edebbb4d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "87\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321856} {:answer/id #uuid "f549df01-be4a-5b50-a029-fa0f1c416859" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "95\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321857} {:answer/id #uuid "b7c22ba1-ff89-52e4-8388-78ef1fe4e335" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "98\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321858} {:answer/id #uuid "ee4eac05-e674-55f3-8197-e2cbcb3b7367" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "89\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321859} {:answer/id #uuid "be41c75a-ea0e-55c3-b887-8e93e3737fea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "67\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321860}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f549df01-be4a-5b50-a029-fa0f1c416859" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "95\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321857} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a0c446b-2ba8-5781-b3f2-0c64025c5166" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321855}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "02ce9872-8986-5e67-bea1-b49d589c24ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-15589" :question/problem "The water boils in a pan at a temperature of ${100}^{∘}C.$ The pan with water is then placed to a room with a temperature of ${25}^{∘}C,$ and the temperature of water decreases. After $5$ minutes, it cools to ${90}^{∘}C.$ To the nearest minute, how long does it take for the water to have a temperature of ${30}^{∘}C?$ \n\n*Hint: You may assume that the temperature reading $T(t)$ of the water in the pan decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling, where $T$ is measured in degrees Celsius and $t>0$ is the time, in minutes, since the water starts cooling.*" :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={25}^{∘}\\text{C},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-25).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-25} & =-k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-25} & =-k\\int dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-25| & =-kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-25>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-25 & ={e}^{-kt+C} \\\\ T-25 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T-25 & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T & =25+K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature was ${100}^{∘}C.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=100,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}100 & =25+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 100 & =25+K \\\\ K & =75\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $T(t):$\n\n$$\nT(t)=25+75{e}^{-kt}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, after $5$ minutes, it cools to ${90}^{∘}C.$ So, applying the condition $T(5)=90,$ we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}90 & =25+75{e}^{-5k} \\\\ 65 & =75{e}^{-5k} \\\\ \\frac{13}{15} & ={e}^{-5k} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{13}{15}) & =-5k \\\\ k & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{13}{15})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the water has a temperature of ${30}^{∘}C.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({t}_{1}) & =30 \\\\ 25+75{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =30 \\\\ 75{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =5 \\\\ {e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{1}{15} \\\\ -k{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{15}) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{15}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in our value for $k,$ we conclude that the temperature of water will be ${30}^{∘}C$ after\n\n$$\n{t}_{1}=\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{15})}{-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{13}{15})}≈95\\,\\text{min},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest minute."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078798 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74a902da-f299-54a3-8e93-ad2716d0e887" :knowledge-point/title "Modeling Inhibited Decay"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b7ccb7df-65a9-5aa4-b3b0-cd2df9e8f72c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dT}{dt} =-k(T - 164)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076000} {:answer/id #uuid "d290b344-74d3-55ac-95ce-b50a12471d73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dT}{dt} =-k(T - 100)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076001} {:answer/id #uuid "d6e1c165-d1cd-53a1-ac94-3acaa07397be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dT}{dt} =-k(T - 64)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076002} {:answer/id #uuid "eeef6b7f-0e0c-596a-b4f1-864ef7a7f25b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dT}{dt} =-64(T - 100)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076003} {:answer/id #uuid "7422c721-2d61-58a6-8a40-a9a8e6e19930" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dT}{dt} =-100(T - 64)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076004}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d6e1c165-d1cd-53a1-ac94-3acaa07397be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dT}{dt} =-k(T - 64)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076002} :answer-field/id #uuid "5be0d10a-eb75-5635-b8ee-f963aee60758" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075999}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "58a15628-6a07-582f-a2d4-e1c7a7c6e77c" :question/math-academy-id "q-37365" :question/problem "A medical doctor records the temperature of ${100}^{∘}\\text{F}$ using a thermometer. After the measurement is taken, the temperature reading $T(t)$ of the thermometer decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling. Here, $T$ is measured in degrees Fahrenheit, and $t>0$ is the time in minutes. If the room temperature is ${64}^{∘}\\text{F},$ which differential equation could be used to model the thermometer reading? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={64}^{∘}\\text{F},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-64).\n$$"}]}
